## 2. Breaking-change notice policy

### 2.1 What counts as breaking

<!-- DECIDE: the actual bar. A defensible starting point: any change that
     requires a consumer to change their own code, config, or expectations to
     keep working, including a behavior change with no accompanying API
     change. Silence on this is what turns "was that breaking?" into a
     recurring argument. -->

### 2.2 How a breaking change is surfaced

At minimum, filled in per release, not left as boilerplate:

- What changed, in terms a consumer (not the author) would recognize
- What breaks as a result, concretely — not "some edge cases"
- The migration step required, if any
- Who to ask if the migration step doesn't work

<!-- DECIDE: the mechanism — a dedicated section in the release notes, a
     major-version bump, a required-reading entry, a direct notification to
     known consumers. Also decide whether a breaking change can ship without
     a version-number signal at all; if it can, name the compensating
     mechanism. -->

